<title>CHILE: Escondida reports soaring copper output in 1996.</title>
<headline>Escondida reports soaring copper output in 1996.</headline>
<dateline>SANTIAGO 1997-01-21</dateline>
<text>
<p>Minera Escondida Ltda reported sharply higher copper output in December and total production of 841,361 tonnes for all of last year, up about 80 percent from 466,913 tonnes in 1995.</p>
<p>The mine officially passed Codelco's Chuquicamata mine, which produces about 620,000 tonnes of copper a year, as the world's largest copper pit last year.</p>
<p>The following are production figures for Escondida, owned by Australia's Broken Hill Proprietary and RTZ Corp Plc:</p>
<p>				December 1996	  December 1995</p>
<p>Copper in concentrates	 76,214		   NA</p>
<p>Copper cathodes		   5,001		   NA</p>
<p> TOTAL			     81,215		 43,250</p>
<p>					1996		    1995</p>
<p>Copper concentrates	   792,272		    NA</p>
<p>Copper cathodes		  49,089		    NA</p>
<p> TOTAL			    841,361		  466,913</p>
<p>     ((--Roger Atwood, Santiago newsroom +56-2-699-5595 x211))</p>
</text>
